From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Elias Bakken <elias@iagent.no>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: drm/bridge: dw-hdmi: debounce spurious HPD disconnect during PHY setup

The sun8i HDMI PHY reports both HPD and RX_SENSE low for ~600ms while it
is being brought up, which dw_hdmi_detect() reports to DRM as a genuine
disconnect. The fbdev client probes connectors inside that window, so it
never reads the EDID and builds the console from a fallback mode the
panel cannot display. The screen stays dark for early boot, and Plymouth
inherits that mode, which is why the boot splash was never visible.

Measured on Recore: plugout at 7.041s, fb0 created at 7.100s, plugin at
7.656s - reproducible to within a millisecond across boots, and
independent of the U-Boot splash and of the HDMI VBUS regulator flags.

Re-read HPD before believing a disconnect. .detect may sleep, and the
loop exits as soon as HPD returns, so the delay is only paid when the
display really is absent.

Signed-off-by: Elias Bakken <elias@iagent.no>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-hdmi.c | 35 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 35 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-hdmi.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-hdmi.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-hdmi.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-hdmi.c
@@ -2451,12 +2451,47 @@
 					  hdmi->rxsense);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The sun8i PHY reports HPD low for roughly 600ms while it is being set up,
+ * even though the sink never went away. Re-read before believing a
+ * disconnect, so a transient during PHY bring-up is not reported to DRM.
+ */
+#define DW_HDMI_HPD_DEBOUNCE_MS		50
+#define DW_HDMI_HPD_DEBOUNCE_TRIES	20
+
 static enum drm_connector_status dw_hdmi_detect(struct dw_hdmi *hdmi)
 {
 	enum drm_connector_status result;
+	unsigned int tries;
 
 	result = hdmi->phy.ops->read_hpd(hdmi, hdmi->phy.data);
 
+	/*
+	 * Debounce a disconnect. This runs from the connector .detect
+	 * callback, which is allowed to sleep.
+	 *
+	 * Without this, the fbdev client probes connectors during the PHY
+	 * bring-up window, sees "disconnected", and so never reads the EDID.
+	 * It then builds the framebuffer console from whatever mode is left -
+	 * on a panel whose EDID it could not read, that is a fallback mode the
+	 * display cannot show, and the screen stays dark for the whole of
+	 * early boot. Plymouth inherits that mode and draws into it, which is
+	 * why the boot splash was invisible.
+	 *
+	 * A genuine unplug is simply reported up to a second later, which
+	 * nothing depends on. The loop exits as soon as HPD returns, so the
+	 * delay is only paid when the display really is disconnected.
+	 */
+	if (result == connector_status_disconnected) {
+		for (tries = 0; tries < DW_HDMI_HPD_DEBOUNCE_TRIES; tries++) {
+			msleep(DW_HDMI_HPD_DEBOUNCE_MS);
+
+			result = hdmi->phy.ops->read_hpd(hdmi, hdmi->phy.data);
+			if (result != connector_status_disconnected)
+				break;
+		}
+	}
+
 	if (result != hdmi->last_connector_result) {
 		dev_info(hdmi->dev, "read_hpd result: %d", result);
 		handle_plugged_change(hdmi,
